{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the beginning Elohim created the heavens and the earth.</b> The reason the Torah begins with a bet is that throughout the book of Ecclesiastes, King Solomon likens the Torah to the sun, whose course runs only in three directions, like the shape of the open bet facing north, and its giving through Moses, whose face was like the sun. And perhaps the reason is that the evil inclination, which opposes the Torah, comes from the north and breaches the fence of the Torah, as it says (Joel 2:20), ״I will remove the northern one far from you,״ and likewise the gold that comes from the north (see Genesis 2:12) opposes the Torah, and for the most part they flee from one another, as it says (Psalms 119:71), ״It is good for me that I was afflicted, so that I might learn Your statutes.״ And this is not the place to speak of this further. And one may explain further, on the basis of what is said (Proverbs 9:1), ״Wisdom has built her house,״ therefore the wisdom of the Torah begins with a bet, for she is the mistress of the universal house." "<b>In the beginning Elohim created.</b> It would have been fitting for the Torah to begin with the name 'Elohim,' so much so that our Rabbis of blessed memory (Megillah 9a) needed to change it for King Ptolemy and write 'Elohim created in the beginning.' And on this the commentators gave a reason in praise: because the existence of the Name, blessed be He, cannot be grasped except through His ways and deeds and the works of His hands which He created — they testify, they declare, they give faithful testimony to His existence, blessed be He. Therefore He stated first 'In the beginning He created' and afterward 'Elohim' was made known. And the difficulty on this is: it should have been written 'Elohim created in the beginning,' for whatever can place the Name first, the rule would give to place it first, since the existence of the Name does not depend on knowledge of the things preceding in creation, to which the word 'בְּרֵאשִׁית' points according to the opinion of most commentators." "And furthermore, the author of the Akeidah based this interpretation on the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 1:12) that says ״Your humility makes me great״ (Psalms 18:36). A king of flesh and blood mentions his name first and afterward his deeds, but the Holy One, blessed be He — once He mentions His deeds, He mentions His name. And one should ponder: what is His humility in this? And what is this that it says 'makes me great' — in what did He make us many and make us great? And it seems to me that Rashi wanted to set all this right with what he said: 'The verse says nothing but 'expound me' — for the sake of the Torah and for the sake of Israel, which are called רֵאשִׁית,' etc. That is to say, since it is impossible to know the existence of the Name, blessed be He, except through the Torah and through Israel, therefore they are called רֵאשִׁית, for both of them are the prerequisite through which one comes to recognition of the existence of the Name, blessed be He — for the Torah proclaims the belief in creation anew and the divinity of the Name, blessed be He. And Israel too knows how to proclaim the existence of the Name, blessed be He, from the side of the tradition they received, each from the mouth of the next, back to Adam the first man, who saw the world destroyed and built up — and he is a faithful witness to the world's being created anew, which is a marvel testifying to the One who renewed it. And this tradition spread through the seed of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel." "And there is yet a third way, but not every person can grasp it, and it is to recognize the existence of the Name, blessed be He, from within philosophical investigation and knowledge of all existent beings in all three worlds. And this is their order: first, the investigator must contemplate the nature of the existent beings in the lower world, for that is the easiest investigation in the world. And after knowing their nature, he should contemplate as well the nature of the created beings in the middle world. And from there he ascends by way of the ladder of the chain of rungs to knowledge of the nature of the upper world. And from there he ascends to know and to grasp that there is an Elohim who exists, riding upon them all, who arranged them in this manner — like the vision that Jacob saw in the four sides of the ladder that was ״set upon the earth״ (Genesis 28:12) — that is, grasping the lower world. ״And its top reaching to heaven״ — this is the middle world. ״And behold, angels of Elohim ascending and descending upon it״ — this is the upper world. ״And behold, YHWH stood above it״ — for then he attained the perfection of knowledge of the existence of the Name, blessed be He." "However, this path of investigation — the many are not wise enough to enter the secret of Elohim while man passes through the turbid valley of this material world. Therefore it suffices for a person when he grasps the existence of the Name, blessed be He, from the side of tradition, as stated, and from the side of the Torah, as stated. And on this our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Chullin 91a) that the beings below mention the Name, blessed be He, after two words, as it says (Deuteronomy 6:4), ״Hear, O Israel, YHWH.״ But the beings above do not mention the Name until after three words, as it says (Isaiah 6:3), ״Holy, holy, holy, YHWH.״ For the beings above — their power of intellect is pure and clean, and it is easy for them to grasp the nature of the investigation of these three worlds; therefore they are not permitted to mention the Name until after three sanctifications, that is, they first apprehend the nature of all lower existents and understand their place of origin, and through this they sanctify the Name, blessed be He, below. And from there they ascend to understand the nature of the existents in the middle world, and they sanctify the Name, blessed be He, to enthrone Him upon them as well. And from there they ascend to understand as well the nature of their own essence, and from there they ascend to grasping His Name, blessed be He. Therefore they do not mention the Name until they have first sanctified Him in all three worlds. But the beings below, whose intellect is not so pure and not every person can grasp this investigation for it is great — therefore it suffices for them to come to recognition of the existence of the Name, blessed be He, after two words: ״Hear, O Israel.״ For ״Hear״ means to hear the words of this Torah that proclaims the divinity of the Name, blessed be He. ״Israel״ refers to the tradition that spread through Israel from Adam the first man to Abraham, who proclaimed and publicized the divinity of the Name, blessed be He, as Rashi explained on the verse ״YHWH, Elohim of heaven, who took me from my father's house״ (Genesis 24:7). And from him the divinity of the Name, blessed be He, was publicized among all the seed of Israel and Judah, as it is written (Psalms 76:2), ״In Judah Elohim is known; in Israel His name is great.״ Therefore it says ״In the beginning He created״ and afterward ״Elohim״ — an allusion to all three, for ״בְּרֵאשִׁית״ means Israel and the Torah, which are called רֵאשִׁית, and through the act of creation Elohim was made known. And this is the way of humility — that He prefaced, in allusion, the name of Israel before His own Name, blessed be He, and this is truly the glory of splendor and greatness for Israel. Therefore it says ״Your humility makes me great.״ And there are those who say that this humility is that He first engaged with the needs of His world and afterward publicized His Name, blessed be He." "<b>Rabbi Yitzḥak said: The Torah did not need to begin, etc., but rather so that the nations should not say to you, 'You are bandits,' etc.</b> And the question is: what does it matter if they say 'you are bandits'? Is it for this that the order of the Torah should change? The answer to this: because this matter causes denial of the fundamental principle, for they will say 'there is no judgment and there is no Judge' and 'whoever is mightier prevails' — for if the world had a Leader who arranges its affairs, its going out and its coming in, why did He not protest when you took with a bandit's arm the lands of the seven nations by force? For on this account the decree of the generation of the Flood was sealed only on account of robbery, as it is said (Proverbs 28:24): ״גּוֹזֵל אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ וְאוֹמֵר אֵין פָּשַׁע, חָבֵר הוּא לְאִישׁ מַשְׁחִית״ — for what makes this sin greater than others is not for the essence of the sin itself but for the evil drawn from it: one who sins by robbery does not lay blame upon himself and says 'there is no transgression,' for there is no Elohim judging on earth, and therefore he is 'a companion to a destructive man' — that is, to the generation of the Flood, concerning whom it is written (Genesis 6:12): ״כִּי הִשְׁחִית כָּל בָּשָׂר אֶת דַּרְכּוֹ״, for they too denied the fundamental principle and said (Job 21:15): ״מַה שַּׁדַּי כִּי נַעַבְדֶנּוּ״ and so forth. And this was the beginning of their doing violence and robbery on the earth. And since the prevention of robbery is the foundation of all faith, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, anticipated it and warned against it in the first commandment, ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה לָכֶם״ (Exodus 12:2), for there it is said ״מִשְׁכוּ וּקְחוּ לָכֶם צֹאן״ (Exodus 12:21) — from what is yours, to remove the stolen property; so explained Ba'al ha-Ṭurim. And likewise Yitzḥak, when he commanded to bring him two kid goats, one to make from it the Paschal offering, said ״וְצוּדָה לִּי צָיִד״ (Genesis 27:3), which Rashi explained: from that which is ownerless and not from robbery. And since Esav's heart would not think thus — but rather to hunt game even by means of robbery — therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, arranged that he did not bring, but rather Ya'akov, who was scrupulous about robbery, did. And likewise the ox that Adam the first man offered — our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Avodah Zarah 8a) that it had one horn on its forehead, to indicate that since Adam the first man was 'one horn,' that is to say, he was unique in the world and did not rob any creature, therefore his offering was accepted. And all this was in order to distance robbery and banditry, which leads to denial of the fundamental principle and heresy." "<b>And what Rabbi Yitzḥak said, 'The Torah did not need to begin,' etc.</b> The meaning is not that the entire narrative from בְּרֵאשִׁית until ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה לָכֶם״ (Exodus 12:2) should not have been written in the Torah at all — for behold, there is great need in the narrative of the creation of the world, for it proclaims the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, and the narrative of the generation of the Flood proclaims His providence, blessed be He, in reward and punishment, apart from the several commandments written within it: the limb of a living animal given to Noaḥ, circumcision to Avraham, and the displaced sinew by the hand of Ya'akov. Rather, what he meant to say is: since the Torah was given principally for its commandments, if so, the Torah should have begun with the first commandment, and this narrative from בְּרֵאשִׁית until ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה לָכֶם״ would have sufficed to be set down at the end of the Torah. To this he comes to respond and says: had He written thus, it would have been a fundamental flaw from the outset, and it would have given a handle to the transgressors to deny the Torah, and upon reading ״הַחֹדֶשׁ הַזֶּה לָכֶם״ all the more would they deny the more, and they would say: 'Surely Moshe invented this commandment from his own heart, for how could HaShem have commanded therein regarding robbery, saying 'Draw out and take for yourselves' specifically from what is yours — and behold you are bandits, for you took the lands of the seven nations and HaShem did not protest against you!' — for perhaps they would not read the Torah from beginning to end. Therefore it was necessary to precede the narrative of the creation of the world, to indicate that it was not by means of banditry that they took the lands, but rather with justice and in judgment. This is what Scripture says (Psalms 111:6–7): ״כֹּחַ מַעֲשָׂיו הִגִּיד לְעַמּוֹ לָתֵת לָהֶם נַחֲלַת גּוֹיִם, מַעֲשֵׂה יָדָיו אֱמֶת וּמִשְׁפָּט, נֶאֱמָנִים כָּל פִּקּוּדָיו״ — for the principal reason for this proclamation, that He declared the power of His deeds to His people, was all in order to indicate that the work of His hands is truth and judgment: that the Torah and the Tablets are the work of Elohim, and the righteous who were created with two hands (cf. Shokher Tov on Psalms 139:5) — all is with truth and judgment, which is what prevents banditry, and 'faithful are all His commandments' — the commandments which HaShem ordained upon the lands of the seven nations, for by His will, blessed be He, He gave it to them, and by His will, blessed be He, He took it from them, and the King, HaShem, appointed other officers over it." "And according to its plain sense: the Torah had to begin from בְּרֵאשִׁית to proclaim the belief that the world was created anew, for this is the foundation of the whole Torah — for were it granted that the world is primordial, there is no room for the receiving of the Torah, which is built on the foundation of free choice. Therefore the foundation had to be laid first."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:32:06.645398151Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}